<p>Use the following procedure to print with black and one of CMY color 
 toners.</p>
<p>This method is useful when you want to improve the readability of documents 
 or to emphasize specific points, except when printing data with photographs, 
 which should be left true to the original. Printing in two colors saves 
 printing costs and enables document creation with effective usage of colors.<br/>
If you want to print with only specific CMYK color(s), use the <a href="~/Topics/PS_CS/IDH_HOW_CMYK"><span class="Term-Soft">Separate into CMYK, then Print in Designated Color Only</span></a> 
 function.</p>
<p>Depending on the printer you are using, this function may not be available.</p>
<p><img alt="" class="help-img" height="74" loading="lazy" src="~/images/help/Two_color.gif" width="55"/></p>
<p>Example: When <span class="Term-Soft">Black &amp; Cyan</span> is selected</p>
<p class="Note">Note:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>This function is unavailable with black and white 
 printers.</p></li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li><p>Open the printing preferences dialog box after 
 creating a document in an application.</p></li>
<li><p>Click the <span class="Term-Soft">Frequently Used 
 Settings</span> tab or the <span class="Term-Soft">Basic</span> menu on 
 the <span class="Term-Soft">Detailed Settings</span> tab.</p></li>
<li><p>Select <span class="Term-Soft">Color</span> in the 
 <span class="Term-Soft">Color/ Black and White:</span> list.</p></li>
<li><p>Click the <span class="Term-Soft">Print Quality:Standard</span> 
 menu on the <span class="Term-Soft">Detailed Settings</span> tab.</p></li>
<li><p>Select <span class="Term-Soft">Advanced Settings</span> 
 in the <span class="Term-Soft">Adjust Image Quality/Color</span>.</p></li>
<li><p>Select a combination of toners to be used for 
 printing in <span class="Term-Soft">Two-color:</span> list.</p></li>
<li><p>After making any other necessary settings, click 
 <span class="Term-OS">OK</span> to close the printing preferences dialog 
 box.</p></li>
<li><p>Start printing.</p></li>
</ol>
